                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 902,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1266,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "The inverse of t.stft: rebuilds a signal from its complex spectrogram. On its own it gives back the original; its use is to change the spectrogram first - silence some frequencies, keep only others, scale them - and hear or see the result: filtering that can change over time, noise removal, separating a tremor from a movement.\n\nThe spectrogram must be the complex output of t.stft (output set to complex), made with the same hop, window shape and center setting given here. The window size is read from the spectrogram itself.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.istft <hop>\nDefault: hop 0 (a quarter of the window, as t.stft).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrogram:\nA complex spectrogram from t.stft: frequency bins x windows, or channels x bins x windows. Every input sends.\n\nlength (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples to make; 0 (the default) gives as many as the windows cover. Set it to the original length to get exactly that back.\n\nhop (int box, also an inlet):\nThe hop t.stft used.\n\nwindow shape, center:\nOptions, under the node: as set on t.stft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe rebuilt samples.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA wave (made in the make_wave subpatcher; double-click it to look inside) taken apart by t.stft and put back together by t.istft: the two plots are the same.\n\nRELATED:\nt.stft makes the spectrogram. t.irfft inverts the spectrum of a whole signal. scipy.filtfilt and the Butterworth filters filter without a spectrogram.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1250,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "The inverse of t.stft: rebuilds a signal from its complex spectrogram. On its own it gives back the original; its use is to change the spectrogram first - silence some frequencies, keep only others, scale them - and hear or see the result: filtering that can change over time, noise removal, separating a tremor from a movement.\n\nThe spectrogram must be the complex output of t.stft (output set to complex), made with the same hop, window shape and center setting given here. The window size is read from the spectrogram itself.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.istft <hop>\nDefault: hop 0 (a quarter of the window, as t.stft).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrogram:\nA complex spectrogram from t.stft: frequency bins x windows, or channels x bins x windows. Every input sends.\n\nlength (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many samples to make; 0 (the default) gives as many as the windows cover. Set it to the original length to get exactly that back.\n\nhop (int box, also an inlet):\nThe hop t.stft used.\n\nwindow shape, center:\nOptions, under the node: as set on t.stft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe rebuilt samples.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA wave (made in the make_wave subpatcher; double-click it to look inside) taken apart by t.stft and put back together by t.istft: the two plots are the same.\n\nRELATED:\nt.stft makes the spectrogram. t.irfft inverts the spectrum of a whole signal. scipy.filtfilt and the Butterworth filters filter without a spectrogram."
                },
